761,11,2176,29136,28370,1191,400,87,61,17,10,19,87,41715,4385,2412,12947,576,11341,315,279,49319,517,11,400,65,61,17,12,19,580,54876,315,16247,594,47311,311,279,49319,517,315,14927,594,47311,374,6144,311,279,11341,315,14927,594,6783,4647,311,16247,594,6783,4647,13,1416,862,6783,3793,525,537,6144,11,1477,279,2629,315,279,6783,3793,382,37314,9960,510,27,37420,91339,397,22043,429,2176,29136,28370,1191,448,17767,87,61,17,488,220,19,87,488,1124,4385,2412,59,701,1077,594,78064,16247,594,47311,438,17767,87,61,17,488,220,19,87,488,264,57758,323,14927,594,47311,438,17767,87,61,17,488,220,19,87,488,293,59,568,576,49319,517,315,264,79151,47311,17767,87,61,17,488,220,19,87,488,272,57758,374,2661,553,17767,19,61,17,481,220,19,1124,50853,220,16,1124,50853,272,284,220,16,21,481,220,19,66,59,568,15277,11,279,49319,517,315,16247,594,47311,374,17767,16,21,481,220,19,64,57758,323,279,49319,517,315,14927,594,47311,374,17767,16,21,481,220,19,65,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nEric and Charles each think of a quadratic polynomial. To their surprise, both quadratics start $x^2+4x+\\cdots$. The ratio of the discriminant, $b^2-4ac$, of Eric's polynomial to the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. If their constant terms are not equal, find the sum of the constant terms.\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nGiven that both quadratics start with \\(x^2 + 4x + \\cdots\\), let's denote Eric's polynomial as \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\) and Charles's polynomial as \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\). The discriminant of a quadratic polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + c\\) is given by \\(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot c = 16 - 4c\\). Therefore, the discriminant of Eric's polynomial is \\(16 - 4a\\) and the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is \\(16 - 4b\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"fb4b0a0736fe9a2c2dc434744c12f9ce34a68ad090bd3c525a60a9a880a67a4d"},"seconds":19.579893589019775,"seed":569291319002421912,"stop":"eos","text":"Given the ratio of the discriminants of Eric's and Charles's polynomials is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side by factoring out the common factor of 4 in the numerator and denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4(4 - a)}{4(4 - b)} = \\frac{b}{a} \\Rightarrow \\frac{4 - a}{4 - b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to eliminate the fraction:\n\n\\[\na(4 - a) = b(4 - b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\n\\[\n4a - a^2 = 4b - b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\nb^2 - a^2 = 4b - 4a\n\\]\n\nFactoring both sides:\n\n\\[\n(b - a)(b + a) = 4(b - a)\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(a \\neq b\\), we can divide both sides by \\(b - a\\) (since \\(b - a \\neq 0\\)):\n\n\\[\nb + a = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the constant terms \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Given the ratio of the discriminants of Eric's and Charles's polynomials is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side by factoring out the common factor of 4 in the numerator and denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4(4 - a)}{4(4 - b)} = \\frac{b}{a} \\Rightarrow \\frac{4 - a}{4 - b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to eliminate the fraction:\n\n\\[\na(4 - a) = b(4 - b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\n\\[\n4a - a^2 = 4b - b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\nb^2 - a^2 = 4b - 4a\n\\]\n\nFactoring both sides:\n\n\\[\n(b - a)(b + a) = 4(b - a)\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(a \\neq b\\), we can divide both sides by \\(b - a\\) (since \\(b - a \\neq 0\\)):\n\n\\[\nb + a = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the constant terms \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]","id":"algebra:1149"}
